{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":515860,"duration_api_ms":550422,"num_turns":26,"result":"","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"e57975d7-051b-48fe-9d88-e5a0ccb4fcf4","total_cost_usd":3.6118822500000003,"usage":{"input_tokens":386,"cache_creation_input_tokens":66926,"cache_read_input_tokens":884513,"output_tokens":4486,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":66926,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":443,"outputTokens":23280,"cacheReadInputTokens":3292547,"cacheCreationInputTokens":221023,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":3.6118822500000003,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"summary":"Reviewed the cycle 2 round 2 changes for tanksim-6e5.6.6: 17 lines of test logic changes to e2e_shrimp_scenario_probes.rs (molt timer seeding + molt_failure_signal assertion in chemistry_stress), 377 lines of param_meta provenance annotations across 4 TOML files, and a 140-line PROVENANCE_STATUS.md summary doc. All 6 tests pass. TOML param_meta entries are well-structured — all keys match real parameters, all valid_ranges are consistent, and all current values fall within their ranges. The substantive test changes (chemistry stress molt-timer seeding and the new molt_failure_signal guard) are correct and improve probe fidelity. Two issues found worth addressing.","amendments":[{"body":"In e2e_shrimp_scenario_probes.rs, the doc comment for probe_successful_breeding (line 183) says \"Over 1 200 simulated hours (~50 days)\" but the implementation uses total_days = 60 (line 211), which is 1,440+ simulated hours. Update the doc comment to say \"Over 1 400 simulated hours (~60 days)\" to match the actual implementation.","rationale":"Doc comment / code mismatch. The comment was likely written when total_days was 50 and not updated when the duration was increased. Since these doc comments serve as the human-readable husbandry stories per the acceptance criteria, they should accurately describe what the test does."},{"body":"In the thermal_scenario_state fixture (e2e_shrimp_scenario_probes.rs ~line 427), the cool control arm finishes with condition 0.267, molt_stress 1.000, failed_molt_accum 1.000, and NO2 = 1.71 mg/L — a severely degraded state, not the healthy baseline the doc comment describes. Root cause: the AOB:NOB biomass ratio (2.0g:1.5g at equal Vmax 10.0) creates a structural nitrite bottleneck, and failed_molt_mortality_scale is not zeroed despite shrimp_base_mortality_per_day and shrimp_stress_mortality_scale being set to 0.0. The probe passes by accident (cool arm produces 8 juveniles before collapsing). Fix by either (a) increasing NOB biomass to >= AOB throughput (e.g., NOB 2.0g), (b) also zeroing shrimp_params.failed_molt_mortality_scale in the thermal fixture (as done in breeding_success_state at line 388), or (c) adding periodic water changes to control nitrite — any of which would make the cool arm a genuine healthy control and make the thermal comparison robust to retuning.","rationale":"The probe claims to isolate thermal effects on reproduction, but the cool control arm is not actually healthy — it is experiencing nitrogen-cycle-driven molt failure and mortality. The directional assertions pass only because the cool arm's different pathology happens to be slightly less reproductively suppressive than heat stress. This makes the probe fragile under parameter retuning and scientifically misleading as a thermal isolation test. The breeding_success_state fixture already demonstrates the fix pattern (zeroing failed_molt_mortality_scale at line 388 and using stronger NOB + water changes)."}]},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"20b04588-1b79-4973-a576-bf1cb39791ab"}
